WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.093
To be, or not to be, that is the question:

00:00:01.093 --> 00:00:02.344
whether 'tis nobler in the mind to suffer

00:00:02.344 --> 00:00:03.368
the slings and arrows
of outrageous fortune,

00:00:03.368 --> 00:00:04.489
or to take arms against a sea of troubles,

00:00:04.489 --> 00:00:05.528
and by opposing end
them: to die, to sleep,

00:00:05.528 --> 00:00:06.815
to sleep, perchance to dream; ah, the ...

00:00:06.815 --> 00:00:07.648
You get the idea.

00:00:07.648 --> 00:00:09.162
The MicroMachine Man, here,
presenting a genuine original,

00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:10.606
colossally collectible, most
midget miniature replicas

00:00:10.606 --> 00:00:11.948
of the real things ...

00:00:11.948 --> 00:00:13.926
MicroMachines!

00:00:13.926 --> 00:00:16.076
A lot of people know me
as the MicroMachine Man,

00:00:16.076 --> 00:00:17.804
or the FedEx guy, or "Terrible" Testeverde

00:00:17.804 --> 00:00:20.055
from Saved by the Bell, or
just that guy who talks fast.

00:00:20.055 --> 00:00:21.240
In fact, I was in the Guinness Book

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.225
as the world's fastest talker,

00:00:22.225 --> 00:00:24.219
and so, for the last 40 years,

00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:28.934
if you needed a fast talker,
I was your go-to guy.

00:00:28.934 --> 00:00:30.043
Is that okay, did you
get everything you need?

00:00:30.043 --> 00:00:31.207
I mean, I can repeat it again if you want.

00:00:31.207 --> 00:00:32.439
You sure you don't want me to do it again?

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.022
Because I could do it again ...

00:00:36.394 --> 00:00:39.394
(stopwatch ticking)

00:00:40.656 --> 00:00:41.982
When I was 12 years old,

00:00:41.982 --> 00:00:46.646
I wanted to get into the
Guinness Book of World Records.

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:48.036
So I figured the only
thing I could really do,

00:00:48.036 --> 00:00:50.923
since I wasn't gonna eat a
car or swallow a lead pipe,

00:00:50.923 --> 00:00:53.789
was to lock myself in a room
and teach myself fast talking.

00:00:53.789 --> 00:00:55.992
And I did that with the Hamlet soliloquy.

00:00:55.992 --> 00:00:56.825
To be or, or not to be,
that is the question.

00:00:56.825 --> 00:00:57.709
To be or, or not to be,
that is the question.

00:00:57.709 --> 00:01:00.392
(overlapping speech)

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:03.146
Years later, I'm at this
party, fancy California,

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:05.089
Beverly Hills party, and a friend of mine

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:06.998
is trying to pick up this Valley girl.

00:01:06.998 --> 00:01:09.120
He's from New York and
talking pretty quickly,

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.549
and she looks at him and says,

00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:11.987
"Like, oh my gosh, are you, like,

00:01:11.987 --> 00:01:14.015
"the world's fastest talker?"

00:01:14.015 --> 00:01:17.599
And he says, "No, as a matter
of fact, this person is."

00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:18.432
They called me over and I did

00:01:18.432 --> 00:01:19.609
one of my little party routines.

00:01:19.609 --> 00:01:22.822
(mumbles tongue twister)

00:01:22.822 --> 00:01:24.684
And a guy walked over, and
he says, "That's incredible!

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:27.366
"I'm a producer, and I'm
gonna put you on a show."

00:01:27.366 --> 00:01:29.108
I went on the show, and
I did the fast-talking,

00:01:29.108 --> 00:01:30.144
thinking that would be it,

00:01:30.144 --> 00:01:31.592
and all of a sudden the
phone starts ringing.

00:01:31.592 --> 00:01:33.196
I'm booked all over the place,

00:01:33.196 --> 00:01:35.098
and I manage to make a living out of it!

00:01:35.098 --> 00:01:38.926
(lively percussion)

00:01:38.926 --> 00:01:40.593
I can speak as fast as 11 words a second.

00:01:40.593 --> 00:01:43.085
Now, I never do 11 words a second for TV,

00:01:43.085 --> 00:01:45.067
but let me do Peter Piper
at 11 words a second.

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:48.484
(mumbles tongue twister)

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.730
You can kinda hear me going puh-puh-puh,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.307
but that's really all
you're kinda getting.

00:01:52.307 --> 00:01:54.779
But when you do TV, there's
visual reinforcement;

00:01:54.779 --> 00:01:56.326
people see you and do whatever,

00:01:56.326 --> 00:01:58.508
so I do about seven to
eight words a second.

00:01:58.508 --> 00:01:59.425
That would sound something like ...

00:01:59.425 --> 00:02:00.695
Peter Piper picked a
peck of pickled peppers;

00:02:00.695 --> 00:02:02.292
a peck of pickled pepper
did Peter Piper pick;

00:02:02.292 --> 00:02:03.413
if Peter Piper picked a
peck of pickled pepper,

00:02:03.413 --> 00:02:04.895
where's the peck of pickled
peppers Peter Piper picked?

00:02:04.895 --> 00:02:07.800
Now, on radio, I have to
do it even a little slower,

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.398
because you have no visual reinforcement;

00:02:09.398 --> 00:02:10.436
you're not seeing me.

00:02:10.436 --> 00:02:12.801
So then I do about five
to six words a second,

00:02:12.801 --> 00:02:14.167
so it sounds something like ...

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.263
Peter Piper picked a
peck of pickled peppers;

00:02:15.263 --> 00:02:16.677
a peck of pickled pepper
did Peter Piper pick;

00:02:16.677 --> 00:02:17.933
if Peter Piper picked a
peck of pickled pepper,

00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:19.995
where's the peck of pickled
peppers Peter Piper picked?

00:02:19.995 --> 00:02:22.950
And that's kinda the secret
to my success, because ...

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.685
if you can't understand what I'm saying,

00:02:25.685 --> 00:02:28.168
they're not gonna pay me the big bucks.

00:02:28.168 --> 00:02:31.132
(frenetic blips)

00:02:31.132 --> 00:02:32.582
Then, the very first commercial I did

00:02:32.582 --> 00:02:35.431
was the Federal Express commercial.

00:02:35.431 --> 00:02:36.264
Okay, you, there's travel plans.

00:02:36.264 --> 00:02:37.232
I need to be in New York
on Monday, LA on Tuesday,

00:02:37.232 --> 00:02:38.065
New York on Wednesday, LA on Thursday ...

00:02:38.065 --> 00:02:40.147
It is, to this day, the most
award-winning commercial

00:02:40.147 --> 00:02:41.685
in the history of advertising.

00:02:41.685 --> 00:02:45.117
The final scene, I'm
sitting behind a desk,

00:02:45.117 --> 00:02:46.781
and I'm going, you know,
"Dave, what's the deal

00:02:46.781 --> 00:02:47.614
"with the deal, are we dealing?

00:02:47.614 --> 00:02:48.447
"Dave, it's a deal with Don.

00:02:48.447 --> 00:02:49.280
"If it's a deal with Doc.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.113
It's a deal, good, I'm putting you down.

00:02:50.113 --> 00:02:51.029
It's a deal with Dick;
Dick, what's deal with ...

00:02:51.029 --> 00:02:54.211
So I did the thing like 29
times without making a mistake.

00:02:54.211 --> 00:02:55.368
And the director said to me,

00:02:55.368 --> 00:02:56.287
"What, are you like a machine?

00:02:56.287 --> 00:02:57.457
"You never make a mistake?

00:02:57.457 --> 00:02:58.350
I said, "You want a mistake?

00:02:58.350 --> 00:02:59.831
"I'll give you a mistake the next time."

00:02:59.831 --> 00:03:02.520
So I said, "Dave, it's a dork
with Dick, Don, and duck."

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.401
And that's the take he ended
up putting in the commercial.

00:03:04.401 --> 00:03:05.359
It's a deal with Dave, Dick, and Dave.

00:03:05.359 --> 00:03:06.483
Don, it's a dock with
Dick, Dave, and duck.

00:03:06.483 --> 00:03:07.723
(tape rewinding)

00:03:07.723 --> 00:03:09.269
Don it's a dock with Dick, Dave, and duck.

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:10.124
The first time I saw it, I called him.

00:03:10.124 --> 00:03:13.457
He said, "John, it's our little secret."

00:03:15.008 --> 00:03:17.375
Although I'm known as the
world's fastest talker,

00:03:17.375 --> 00:03:22.003
I do not choose to live my
entire life in the fast lane.

00:03:22.003 --> 00:03:25.913
Sometimes I just like to
take it all a little slower.

00:03:25.913 --> 00:03:27.332
But sometimes it's really,
really, really good

00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:28.249
to be fast.

00:03:29.233 --> 00:03:32.983
(cheerful electronic music)

